<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stonehenge</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 3100–1600 BCE</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Stonehenge is a prehistoric megalithic monument on Salisbury Plain in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wiltshire">Wiltshire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, consisting of an outer ring of sarsen standing stones topped by lintels, an inner ring of bluestones and free-standing trilithons. It was built in phases from about 3100 BCE, when the surrounding bank and ditch were dug, until about 1600 BCE; the large sarsen circle was placed between 2600 and 2400 BCE. The monument is aligned on the summer solstice sunrise and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winter-solstice">winter solstice</a> sunset, and human bone deposits suggest it may have been a burial ground. It became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in 1986.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stonehenge.jpg" alt="Stonehenge" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: garethwiscombe · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stonehenge2007_07_30.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Stonehenge was built in several phases over at least 1,500 years by a culture that left no written records. Beneath the site of a former car park, archaeologists found large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesolithic">Mesolithic</a> postholes dated to around 8000 BCE. The first monument, about 3100 BCE, was a circular bank and ditch about 110 m across, with a large entrance to the north-east and a smaller one to the south. Deer and ox bones and worked flint tools were placed in the ditch. Inside the enclosure is a circle of 56 pits called the Aubrey holes, after the 17th-century antiquarian John Aubrey.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Around 2600 BCE the builders abandoned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> for stone and dug holes for up to 80 standing bluestones, of which only 43 can be traced. It is generally accepted that the bluestones were brought from the Preseli Hills in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pembrokeshire">Pembrokeshire</a>, 240 km away. From 2600 to 2400 BCE 30 enormous sarsens were brought from West Woods in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wiltshire">Wiltshire</a>, to the north, dressed and fitted with mortise and tenon joints. They were raised in a circle about 30 m across with a ring of lintels on top, joined by tongue and groove joints, a woodworking method.</p><p>In later phases the bluestones were re-erected and rearranged, in a circle between the two rings of sarsens and in an oval at the centre, and the Bluestone Horseshoe was made between 1930 and 1600 BCE. The Y and Z Holes of about 1600 BCE are the last known construction. The main axis points to the summer solstice sunrise and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winter-solstice">winter solstice</a> sunset. Human bone deposits date from as early as 3000 BCE, so the site may have been a burial ground from its beginnings.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inigo-jones">Inigo Jones</a> thought the monument was built in Roman times, and John Aubrey, in 1666, was among the first to examine it scientifically. Aubrey and William Stukeley both linked it to Druidism. John Wood the Elder made the most accurate early plan in 1740, before the south-west trilithon collapsed in 1797 and was restored in 1958. Stonehenge was auctioned on 21 September 1915, and Cecil Chubb bought it for £6,600 and gave it to the nation three years later. It became a scheduled monument in 1882 and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in 1986, and is managed by English Heritage. DNA studies suggest that the builders of the earliest phases descended mostly from farmers from the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Stonehenge” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its plan and size closely match those of <b>Stonehenge</b>, which was much elaborated in this period, and an enclosure of about 2000 BCE near Tiel in the Netherlands had entrances aligned to the solstices and equinoxes.</blockquote><a href="/culture/bell-beaker-culture">Bell Beaker culture · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>The Wessex culture of southern Britain, with its rich barrow burials near <b>Stonehenge</b> from around 2000 BCE, the gold and bronze of Bronze Age Denmark and the princely graves of the Hallstatt culture, such as the Hochdorf Chieftain’s Grave of about 530 BCE, are widely read as the…</blockquote><a href="/government/chiefdom">Chiefdom · Through History</a></li></ul></section>
